    "created_at": "2020-11-29T02:06:11Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-11-29T02:06:11Z",
    "body": "> I remember some talk about having `starship init` autodetect the shell, so detecting nu from the starship side might also be possible.\r\n\r\nFun fact: back in the old days (like, before we even had dedicated init scripts) `starship init` actually used to autodetect the shell. \r\n\r\nUnfortunately, there was a pretty nasty bug caused by the fact that the shell name as recorded by the shell sometimes does weird stuff. In our case, sometimes the shell names for zsh and bash showed up as `-zsh` and `-bash`, which `clap` was incapable of parsing as a raw string instead of an attempted flag to starship.\r\n\r\nI think the argument parsing libraries have come a long way since then, so maybe it'd be possible to re-insert shell autodetection. The key problem to solve, IIRC,  is that we somehow need the following to parse correctly:\r\n\r\n`starship init --shell -zsh`\r\n\r\nWe need our argparser to understand that `-zsh` is a string for `--shell` and not a short flag so that we can actually get the string into starship and interpret it as a shell name. Previously, you'd get something like `-zsh: invalid flag` from clap.",
